@page "/learn/circuit-auto-pause"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Pause hidden tabs · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<style>
    .autopause-bad { color: var(--bad); }
</style>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Pause hidden tabs</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Staff leave tabs open all day. Why does each hidden one still use memory on your server?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> the AutoPause package. It pauses a hidden tab for you, but only when it's safe.
        .NET 10 could already pause a tab with <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, but you had to write the script
        that calls it yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        With Interactive Server, each open tab has a <i>circuit</i> on the server: its components, their data and a
        live connection. A hidden tab keeps its circuit until it's closed. Pausing frees it. The tab's
        <code>[PersistentState]</code> values are saved in the browser, and put back on a new circuit when the tab
        comes back.
    </p>
    <p>
        The catch is when to pause. Here's a stock take. Posting the count to the warehouse takes 20 seconds. The user
        presses <b>Post count</b>, switches to another tab, and comes back 30 seconds later. In .NET 10 you pause a
        hidden tab with a script of your own. This one pauses once the tab has been hidden for 10 seconds:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 pause script</span>
        <pre class="snip">document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", async () =&gt; {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  if (document.hidden) {
    <span class="err">timer = setTimeout(async () =&gt;
      paused = await Blazor.pauseCircuit(), 10_000);</span>
  } else if (paused) {
    paused = !await Blazor.resumeCircuit();
  }
});</pre>
    </div>
    <p>It doesn't check whether anything is still running. Here's what that does to the stock take:</p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 1 · .NET 10: the user posts the count, then hides the tab for 30 seconds</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h bad">.NET 10 · your own pause script</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li style="--d:0.3s"><b>0 s</b> Post count pressed, tab hidden</li>
                <li style="--d:1.2s"><b>10 s</b> the script pauses the tab <span class="autopause-bad" style="--d:1.5s">saved as "Not posted"</span></li>
                <li style="--d:2.1s"><b>20 s</b> the warehouse gets the count <span class="autopause-bad" style="--d:2.4s">but the tab is already paused</span></li>
                <li style="--d:3s"><b>30 s</b> tab shown again <span class="autopause-bad" style="--d:3.3s">"Not posted"</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:1.2s"><b>Stock take</b><span class="was">posting…</span><span class="now">paused at 10 s</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict bad" style="--d:3.6s">✗ The tab says "Not posted", but the warehouse has the count. The user posts it again.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            The script pauses on the clock, in the middle of the post. The tab is saved before the post finishes, so it
            comes back saying "Not posted".
        </p>
    </figure>

    <p>You can try it. The demo needs about 30 seconds in another tab.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The stock take, paused by your own script" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: your own pause script" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Post count</b>, switch to another tab for about 30 seconds, then come back. It says
            <b>Not posted</b>, but the line under it says the warehouse has the count. The line at the top says
            the tab was paused for about 20 seconds.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The pause lands mid-post</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Posted</code> is only set when the click handler returns, 20 seconds after the click. The script
        paused the tab at 10 seconds, so it saved <code>Posted</code> while it was still empty. The post did finish
        on the server, but the tab had already gone. When the tab comes back, it starts again from what was saved:
        "Not posted".
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Let AutoPause pick the moment</h2>
    <p>
        Add the <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</code> package, turn it on for the page, and
        delete your own script.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="autoPause" /&gt;</span>

@@code {
    static readonly BrowserOptions autoPause = new BrowserOptions()
        <span class="hl">.AddAutoPause(o =&gt; o.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));</span>
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        That's all for one page. If a click is still being handled when the time is up, AutoPause waits for it to
        finish. Then it pauses, with the result saved.
    </p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 3 · .NET 11: the same post, with AutoPause</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h ok">.NET 11 · AutoPause</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li style="--d:0.3s"><b>0 s</b> Post count pressed, tab hidden</li>
                <li style="--d:1.2s"><b>10 s</b> time to pause, but the post isn't done <span class="wait" style="--d:1.5s">… waits</span></li>
                <li style="--d:2.1s"><b>20 s</b> the warehouse gets the count, then the tab pauses <span class="done" style="--d:2.4s">✓ saved as "Posted"</span></li>
                <li style="--d:3s"><b>30 s</b> tab shown again <span class="done" style="--d:3.3s">✓ "Posted at …"</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="tabs">
                <div class="tab paused" style="--d:2.1s"><b>Stock take</b><span class="was">posting…</span><span class="now">paused at 20 s</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict ok" style="--d:3.6s">✓ The tab says "Posted at …". The count is posted once.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            AutoPause waits for the post to finish before it pauses, so the result is saved too.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <DemoFigure Number="4" Title="The same stock take, with AutoPause" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take"
                Label=".NET 11: AutoPause">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Post count</b>, switch to another tab for about 30 seconds, then come back. This
            time it says <b>Posted at</b> a time. The line at the top says the tab was paused for about 10
            seconds, not 20 as in Fig. 2. It paused later, once the post was done.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code behind the two demos.</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="wwwroot/js/auto-pause-net10.js" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's pause script. Highlighted: 10 seconds after the tab hides, it calls
            <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, whatever is going on.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockTake.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 4's page. Highlighted: the new code. It turns on AutoPause with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>,
            which is also new in .NET 11. The count sheet is the same one Fig. 2 uses.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="7" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockPost.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            The post both demos share. Highlighted, but not new: a plain click handler that takes 20 seconds.
            <code>Posted</code> is a <code>[PersistentState]</code> value, but it's only set when the handler returns.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Leave it, or pause it yourself</h2>
    <p>We tried both on .NET 10, on this stock take and on a page where the user is typing a note:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to handle a hidden tab in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// no script</span></pre>
                <span class="why">a hidden tab keeps its circuit</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the server kept the circuit the whole time the tab was hidden</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", …)
<span class="hl">Blazor.pauseCircuit()</span></pre>
                <span class="why">pause it yourself once the tab has been hidden a while</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ freed the circuit; the count and note came back on a new one</div>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ paused whatever was going on: a post still running, as in Fig. 2, or a half-typed note, whose unsent part was lost</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script frees the circuit, but it pauses whatever the user is doing. Checking for that was up to you.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A pause that waits its turn</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the AutoPause package watches the tab for you. Once the tab has been hidden long enough, it checks
        that nothing is in progress. If something is, it checks again every half second. Then it calls the same
        <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, and resumes when the tab comes back. That's what Fig. 4 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It waits for typing too.</b> It won't pause while the user has typed something the server hasn't received yet, so a half-typed note is safe. It also waits while sound is playing, or while Picture-in-Picture or a Web Lock is in use.</li>
        <li><b>Only <code>[PersistentState]</code> survives.</b> A resumed tab gets a new circuit, so components start over and reload their data. The saved state waits in the browser, encrypted.</li>
        <li><b>For every page, use <code>Program.cs</code>:</b> <code>app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;().WithBrowserOptions(o =&gt; o.AddAutoPause())</code>. The default wait is 2 minutes.</li>
        <li><b>Pick one way.</b> If <code>Program.cs</code> calls <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> and a page uses <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>, that page's settings leak to every page, for everyone. So use one or the other, not both. (That's how .NET 11 RC2 behaves; it may change.) This app only uses <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
